Here's a small TypeScript helper. It wraps `localStorage` defensively, since access can throw (private mode, disabled storage, quota exceeded) and stored data can be corrupt.

```ts
const KEY_PREFIX = "draft:v1:";

export interface Draft {
  docId: string;
  content: string;
  savedAt: number;                       // epoch ms
  baseRevision?: string | number | null; // server revision the draft was based on
}

const draftKey = (docId: string) => `${KEY_PREFIX}${docId}`;

function getStorage(): Storage | null {
  try {
    return typeof window !== "undefined" ? window.localStorage : null;
  } catch {
    return null; // access can throw when storage is blocked
  }
}

/** Saves a draft. Returns true on success, false if it couldn't be written. */
export function saveDraft(
  docId: string,
  content: string,
  baseRevision?: string | number | null
): boolean {
  const storage = getStorage();
  if (!storage) return false;

  const draft: Draft = { docId, content, savedAt: Date.now(), baseRevision };
  try {
    storage.setItem(draftKey(docId), JSON.stringify(draft));
    return true;
  } catch (err) {
    // Most likely QuotaExceededError
    console.warn(`Failed to save draft for ${docId}`, err);
    return false;
  }
}

/** Returns the stored draft, or null if none exists or it's unreadable. */
export function loadDraft(docId: string): Draft | null {
  const storage = getStorage();
  if (!storage) return null;

  try {
    const raw = storage.getItem(draftKey(docId));
    if (!raw) return null;

    const parsed = JSON.parse(raw) as Partial<Draft>;
    if (
      typeof parsed?.content !== "string" ||
      typeof parsed?.savedAt !== "number"
    ) {
      storage.removeItem(draftKey(docId)); // discard malformed data
      return null;
    }
    return {
      docId,
      content: parsed.content,
      savedAt: parsed.savedAt,
      baseRevision: parsed.baseRevision ?? null,
    };
  } catch {
    return null;
  }
}

/** Removes the draft, e.g. after a successful save to the server. */
export function clearDraft(docId: string): void {
  try {
    getStorage()?.removeItem(draftKey(docId));
  } catch {
    /* ignore */
  }
}
```

A few design notes:

- **`baseRevision`**: This is optional, but I'd recommend using it. When the user reopens a document, comparing the draft's base revision to the server's current one tells you whether the draft is safe to restore silently or whether you need to ask the user about a conflict.
- **`savedAt`**: This lets you show "Restore draft from 2:14 PM?" and compare against the server's last-modified time.
- **Versioned prefix (`v1`)**: If you later change the draft shape, you can migrate or ignore old entries without crashing.
- **Return value of `saveDraft`**: The autosave loop can use this to show a "couldn't save locally" warning rather than failing silently, which matters when the whole point is preventing lost edits.

When you wire up the 5-second timer, it's worth skipping the write if the content hasn't changed since the last tick, and clearing the draft only after the server confirms a successful save. Want me to write the autosave hook/loop next?

I'm assuming React with TypeScript, since you didn't mention a framework. Let me know if you're on something else.

The indicator takes a `target` prop (`"local"` or `"server"`). Your autosave only writes a local draft at first, and a bare "Saved" would tell users their work is safe on the server when it isn't. That would be a bad message for an app whose problem is lost edits.

```tsx
import { useEffect, useState } from "react";

export type SaveStatus = "idle" | "dirty" | "saving" | "saved" | "error";

export interface SaveIndicatorProps {
  status: SaveStatus;
  /** Epoch ms of the last successful save. */
  savedAt: number | null;
  /** Where the last save went. "local" = browser draft, "server" = persisted. */
  target: "local" | "server";
  className?: string;
}

function formatRelative(savedAt: number, now: number): string {
  const seconds = Math.max(0, Math.floor((now - savedAt) / 1000));
  if (seconds < 10) return "just now";
  if (seconds < 60) return `${seconds}s ago`;
  const minutes = Math.floor(seconds / 60);
  if (minutes < 60) return `${minutes}m ago`;
  return `at ${new Date(savedAt).toLocaleTimeString([], {
    hour: "numeric",
    minute: "2-digit",
  })}`;
}

/** Re-renders on an interval so relative times stay fresh. */
function useNow(enabled: boolean, intervalMs = 5000): number {
  const [now, setNow] = useState(() => Date.now());
  useEffect(() => {
    if (!enabled) return;
    setNow(Date.now());
    const id = setInterval(() => setNow(Date.now()), intervalMs);
    return () => clearInterval(id);
  }, [enabled, intervalMs]);
  return now;
}

export function SaveIndicator({
  status,
  savedAt,
  target,
  className,
}: SaveIndicatorProps) {
  const now = useNow(status === "saved" && savedAt !== null);

  const noun = target === "server" ? "Saved" : "Draft saved locally";

  // Coarse label: this is what screen readers announce on change.
  let label: string;
  switch (status) {
    case "saving":
      label = "Saving…";
      break;
    case "saved":
      label = noun;
      break;
    case "dirty":
      label = "Unsaved changes";
      break;
    case "error":
      label =
        target === "server"
          ? "Couldn't save. Your latest edits may be lost"
          : "Couldn't save a local draft";
      break;
    default:
      label = "";
  }

  if (!label) return null;

  return (
    <span className={className} data-status={status}>
      {/* Only status changes are announced, not the ticking timestamp. */}
      <span role="status" aria-live="polite">
        {label}
      </span>
      {status === "saved" && savedAt !== null && (
        <span
          aria-hidden="true"
          title={new Date(savedAt).toLocaleString()}
        >
          {" "}
          {formatRelative(savedAt, now)}
        </span>
      )}
    </span>
  );
}
```

Usage with the draft helper:

```tsx
<SaveIndicator status={status} savedAt={savedAt} target="local" />
```

Optional styling keyed off `data-status`:

```css
[data-status="saved"]  { color: #5f6b7a; }
[data-status="dirty"]  { color: #8a6d00; }
[data-status="error"]  { color: #b42318; font-weight: 500; }
```

Design notes:

- **Screen reader noise:** The `role="status"` region holds only the label, and the relative time is `aria-hidden`. Otherwise the "12s ago → 17s ago" ticks would be announced repeatedly.
- **Timer only runs when needed:** The interval is active only in the `saved` state, so there's no background re-rendering while idle or saving.
- **Time rounding:** With a 5s tick, the seconds display is approximate, which is fine for this purpose. The `title` attribute shows the exact timestamp on hover.
- **Error state:** It's worded to be unmissable, since a silent failure here defeats the feature. Drive it from the boolean returned by `saveDraft`.
- **Switching to `target="server"`:** Once the server save is wired in, pass `"server"` after a confirmed response. You could also show both, e.g. "Saved · draft synced", if you keep local drafts as a fallback.

